        </div>
        <p style="font-size:11px;color:#8E8B82;margin-top:12px">UPI-heavy mix (55%) means pricing changes are less friction-sensitive — customers don't "feel" the difference in bills as tangibly as with cash. This is favorable for a price increase.</p>

        <h3 style="margin-top:24px">Table Utilization</h3>
        <div style="margin-top:10px">
          <div class="bar-h"><span class="bar-label">Table 1</span><div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" style="width:100%;background:var(--chart1)"></div></div><span class="bar-val">5,268 (60%)</span></div>
          <div class="bar-h"><span class="bar-label">Table 2</span><div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" style="width:30.7%;background:var(--chart2)"></div></div><span class="bar-val">1,619 (19%)</span></div>
          <div class="bar-h"><span class="bar-label">Car Area</span><div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" style="width:17.5%;background:var(--chart3)"></div></div><span class="bar-val">921 (11%)</span></div>
          <div class="bar-h"><span class="bar-label">Table 3–5</span><div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" style="width:16.2%;background:var(--chart4)"></div></div><span class="bar-val">854 (10%)</span></div>
        </div>
        <p style="font-size:11px;color:#8E8B82;margin-top:10px">Table 1 handles 60% of all orders — likely the counter/default. Car area (11%) is a separate channel worth pricing independently.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="chart-card">
      <h3>Items Per Order Distribution</h3>
      <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--text2);margin-top:4px">Average: 1.76 items/order. <strong>53.4% of orders are single-item</strong> — meaning more than half the customers buy just one thing and leave. This is both a pricing vulnerability (low AOV) and a combo opportunity.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- TAB: Pricing Recommendations -->
  <div class="tab-content" id="tab-pricing">
    <div class="insight-box">
      <h4>Industry Context</h4>
      <p>India's food inflation hit 4.38% in June 2026 (Ministry of Statistics data). Restaurant prices nationally rose ~8.2% YoY in Feb 2026, outpacing retail food inflation at 3.3%. The target food cost for Indian restaurants is 28–32%. With ingredient costs rising 6–8% annually, a restaurant that hasn't repriced in the last 6 months is likely absorbing 3–5% margin erosion. A moderate, targeted increase is industry-standard practice in 2026.</p>
    </div>

    <h3 class="section-title" style="margin-top:24px">Recommended Price Changes — Item by Item</h3>

    <table style="margin-bottom:24px">
      <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th class="num">Current</th><th class="num">Proposed</th><th class="num">Increase</th><th>Rationale</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr style="background:#E8F3ED"><td><strong>Desi Indori Poha</strong></td><td class="num">₹50</td><td class="num">₹59</td><td class="num"><span class="badge badge-green">+₹9 (18%)</span></td><td style="font-size:12px">Anchor item, massive volume (2,245 units). ₹59 stays under the ₹60 psychological barrier. Impact: ~₹20K over the period.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Signature Poha</td><td class="num">₹70</td><td class="num">₹79</td><td class="num"><span class="badge badge-green">+₹9 (13%)</span></td><td style="font-size:12px">Second-highest seller. ₹79 is still under ₹80. Est. impact: ~₹12K.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Paneer / Dryfruits / Peri Peri Poha</td><td class="num">₹60</td><td class="num">₹69</td><td class="num"><span class="badge badge-green">+₹9 (15%)</span></td><td style="font-size:12px">Mid-tier poha. Still comfortably under ₹70. Affects ~1,332 units.</td></tr>
        <tr style="background:#FFF3E6"><td><strong>Regular Chai</strong></td><td class="num">₹60</td><td class="num">₹60</td><td class="num"><span class="badge badge-orange">No change</span></td><td style="font-size:12px">Keep as loss leader. Chai is the gateway — customers order poha/snacks alongside. Raising chai risks losing walk-ins.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Large Chai</td><td class="num">₹40</td><td class="num">₹40</td><td class="num"><span class="badge badge-orange">No change</span></td><td style="font-size:12px">Same logic — beverage anchors shouldn't be touched.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Classic Cold Coffee</td><td class="num">₹90</td><td class="num">₹99</td><td class="num"><span class="badge badge-green">+₹9 (10%)</span></td><td style="font-size:12px">High volume (879), premium positioning. ₹99 is a clean price point. Impact: ~₹8K.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Davidoff Cold Coffee</td><td class="num">₹150</td><td class="num">₹169</td><td class="num"><span class="badge badge-green">+₹19 (13%)</span></td><td style="font-size:12px">Premium item with lower elasticity. Buyers are paying for the brand signal.</td></tr>
        <tr style="background:#E8F3ED"><td><strong>T. Paneer Tikka Sandwich</strong></td><td class="num">₹120</td><td class="num">₹139</td><td class="num"><span class="badge badge-green">+₹19 (16%)</span></td><td style="font-size:12px">Top sandwich (425 units). Paneer ingredient costs rose significantly. ₹139 still lands below ₹140.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Other Paneer Sandwiches</td><td class="num">₹110–140</td><td class="num">₹129–159</td><td class="num"><span class="badge badge-green">+₹19</span></td><td style="font-size:12px">Pizza Paneer, Malai Paneer, Cheese Onion — move in lockstep.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Wraps (all)</td><td class="num">₹70–130</td><td class="num">₹79–139</td><td class="num"><span class="badge badge-green">+₹9</span></td><td style="font-size:12px">Wraps have a healthy margin at current prices. Moderate increase.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Burgers (Crispy Veg, Peri Peri)</td><td class="num">₹60–70</td><td class="num">₹69–79</td><td class="num"><span class="badge badge-green">+₹9</span></td><td style="font-size:12px">Entry-level burgers. Keep under ₹80.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Explosive Cheese / T. Tikka Burger</td><td class="num">₹80</td><td class="num">₹89</td><td class="num"><span class="badge badge-green">+₹9</span></td><td style="font-size:12px">Premium burgers can absorb a small bump.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Maggi (all variants)</td><td class="num">₹70–120</td><td class="num">₹79–129</td><td class="num"><span class="badge badge-green">+₹9</span></td><td style="font-size:12px">Maggi is comfort food with loyal buyers. ₹9 won't deter.</td></tr>
        <tr style="background:#FFF3E6"><td><strong>Kulhad Chai (Small) / Small Cup</strong></td><td class="num">₹10–20</td><td class="num">₹10–20</td><td class="num"><span class="badge badge-orange">No change</span></td><td style="font-size:12px">Ultra-low-price items that bring footfall. Don't touch.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="verdict">
      <h3>Bottom Line: What Should the Increase Be?</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>A selective ₹9–19 increase</strong> across food items (not beverages), targeting the top-selling poha, sandwich, and coffee items. This translates to a <strong>blended 8–12% increase</strong> across the menu.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">
        Estimated impact on a monthly basis (assuming current volume holds):
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:8px">
        <span class="highlight-num">₹20,000–30,000/month</span> in additional revenue, which would push daily average revenue from ~₹6,840 to roughly <span class="highlight-num">₹7,700–7,900</span>, and AOV from ₹151 to approximately <span class="highlight-num">₹165–170</span>.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">
        <strong>Strategy:</strong> Use psychological pricing (₹59, ₹79, ₹99, ₹139 — always just under the next round number). Hold chai and entry-level beverages steady as traffic anchors. Phase the changes: poha first (your signature, your right to reprice), then sandwiches/wraps a week later, then coffee/burgers/maggi a week after. Don't change everything on the same day.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- TAB: Qualitative Metrics -->
  <div class="tab-content" id="tab-qualitative">
    <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--text2);margin-bottom:20px">Beyond what this data shows, here are the metrics your friend should track and think about before and after any price change.</p>

    <div class="qual-grid">
      <div class="qual-card" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent)">
        <h4>1. Customer Walkaway Rate</h4>
        <p>After the price increase, observe how many people look at the menu and leave without ordering. This is the single most important qualitative signal. Even a 5% walkaway rate is a red flag for a ₹50-poha customer segment in Kanpur. Have the counter person keep a mental tally for the first 2 weeks.</p>
        <div class="metric">Track: Daily walkaway count for 14 days post-increase</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qual-card" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent)">
        <h4>2. Order Composition Shift</h4>
        <p>Watch if customers start downgrading — ordering plain poha instead of Signature, skipping the chai add-on, or dropping the second item. A drop in avg items/order (currently 1.76) below 1.6 would signal price resistance.</p>
        <div class="metric">Track: Items per order, weekly. Current baseline: 1.76</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qual-card" style="border-left:3px solid var(--orange)">
        <h4>3. Competitor Pricing</h4>
        <p>Kanpur's street food and cafe scene sets a local reference price. If nearby chai-poha spots sell Indori poha at ₹30–40, jumping to ₹59 puts you at a 50–97% premium. That premium is justifiable only if the ambiance, taste, or experience is clearly better. Send someone to check 3–4 nearby competitors' prices.</p>
        <div class="metric">Track: Competitor prices for top 5 items, quarterly</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qual-card" style="border-left:3px solid var(--orange)">
        <h4>4. Zomato / Swiggy Rating & Reviews</h4>
        <p>Even though you're focusing on dine-in, online reviews shape walk-in traffic. Post-increase, monitor for comments mentioning "expensive", "overpriced", or "not worth it". These are early warning signals before you see it in the order numbers.</p>
        <div class="metric">Track: Weekly review sentiment for 4 weeks post-change</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qual-card" style="border-left:3px solid var(--chart3)">
        <h4>5. Repeat Customer Behavior</h4>
        <p>This data doesn't have customer IDs, but regulars are visible in the pattern — the 8 AM poha-chai orders at Table 1 every day. If regulars start coming less frequently or reducing their orders, the increase went too far. Staff should note any verbal complaints from known regulars.</p>
        <div class="metric">Track: Staff feedback on regular customer reactions, weekly</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qual-card" style="border-left:3px solid var(--chart3)">
        <h4>6. Food Cost Percentage</h4>
        <p>The industry target is 28–32% for Indian restaurants. If your friend doesn't track this, the price increase decision is flying blind. Calculate: (monthly ingredient cost ÷ monthly revenue) × 100. If it's above 35%, the increase is overdue. If it's already at 28%, maybe focus on volume instead.</p>
        <div class="metric">Track: Monthly food cost %. Target: 28–32%</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qual-card" style="border-left:3px solid var(--chart4)">
        <h4>7. Monsoon / Seasonal Sensitivity</h4>
        <p>July 2026 saw a 31% drop in daily revenue vs May. If this is monsoon-driven, pricing power is weakest during Jul–Aug. Consider timing the increase for October (post-monsoon, pre-Diwali festive period) when footfall naturally recovers.</p>
        <div class="metric">Track: Monthly revenue trend vs seasonal weather patterns</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qual-card" style="border-left:3px solid var(--chart4)">
        <h4>8. Combo Attach Rate</h4>
        <p>If you introduce named combos (e.g., "Poha + Chai ₹99"), track what percentage of orders use them vs ordering à la carte. A high combo attach rate (>30%) means customers are price-anchoring on the combo, and individual item prices matter less.</p>
        <div class="metric">Track: Combo orders as % of total, post-launch</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qual-card" style="border-left:3px solid var(--red)">
        <h4>9. Staff Morale & Tip Behavior</h4>
        <p>In a small outlet, staff hear customer grumbling first. If they start dreading the "why is it more expensive now?" question, that erodes service quality. Brief the staff on the rationale and give them a simple line: "Ingredient costs have gone up, but we've kept chai the same price."</p>
        <div class="metric">Track: Informal staff check-in, weekly for first month</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qual-card" style="border-left:3px solid var(--red)">
        <h4>10. Revenue per Seat-Hour</h4>
        <p>With Table 1 handling 60% of orders, you're likely constrained by seating. Revenue per seat-hour tells you if the price increase is making each occupied seat more profitable — the real goal. If daily orders drop 5% but revenue rises 8%, the increase is working even if it feels quieter.</p>
        <div class="metric">Track: (Daily revenue ÷ seats ÷ operating hours). Optimize for this, not just footfall.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="footnote">
  Analysis based on 8,718 dine-in orders from 4 Mar – 12 Sep 2026 (excl. 1 bulk order #8485). Delivery/packaging orders excluded. Item-level prices derived from single-item order medians. Revenue estimates are approximate where orders contained multiple items. Industry data sourced from Ministry of Statistics (Jun 2026), Restaurant India, and QSR Media.
</div>

